{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/68558-looking-for-geists-from-an-informed-outsider-s-point-of-view","id":68558,"title":"Looking for \"Geists from an informed outsider's point of view\"","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Geist: The Sin-Eaters"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-02-09T11:36:18-08:00","id":68558,"title":"Looking for \"Geists from an informed outsider's point of view\"","content":"Hello everyone!<br /> <br /> So I was hoping to feature the occasional Krewe in a game I'm running sooner or later, but have only middling familiarity and none of the books. I didn't want to have to retcon anything if a later player wanted to play one of them or get more involved. I'm shooting for a persistent setting (centralized on Huntsville, Alabama, if that makes any difference), and wanted to do them right.<br /> <br /> So what I'm asking for, could someone give me an overview of what kinds of groups there are, what Geists can *do*, and why/when they do it. I'd like to involve more groups than just the &quot;good guys&quot; (if there are any), I don't want to limit myself to the stereotypically &quot;Player&quot; groups or &quot;Enemy&quot; groups.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Once is happenstance. Twice is coincidence. Three times is enemy action. Four times is Infrastructure.<br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><br /> Rule #2: Never let the game-mechanics get in the way of a good story.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1642-paddon","name":"Paddon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1642_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"326"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-09T13:16:19-08:00","id":68612,"title":"","content":"The first big thing is they tend to be called &quot;Sin-Eaters&quot; or &quot;The Bound&quot;. Some groups may call them &quot;Ghostwalkers&quot;. &quot;Geists&quot; are what are in their head.<br /> <br /> Sin-Eaters are organized into Krewes. Krewes have tiers like Hunters do. First Tier are like a group of PCs, Second are like a city organization, Third is a national sized one. There are no known Third currently, but there have been in the past and there could be one most Sin-Eaters don't know about. But unlike most other games, Sin-Eaters have no groups like Courts or Covenants...Krewes are more-or-less unique. Krewes have their own mythologies, eithoses, duties, and bans. Sin-Eaters do communicate via the &quot;Twilight Network&quot;, a sort of hobo sign system that has blossomed somewhat on the internet, so compared to many WoD critters they are in communication with each other. Sin-Eaters are rarer than Vampires or Changelings or Werewolves, which is part of why they are so disorganized. Also the fact they come into being wholly independent of each other (anyone with some connection to death dying can form one with no contact with others).<br /> <br /> All Sin-Eaters have an powerful ghost/death spirit called a Geist in their head. Once bonded, it does not really interact beyond talking to the Sin-Eater and any other Geists in the Krewe via what is called a channel. Their Morality stat has a lot to do with how good a terms they remain on with their Geist.<br /> <br /> As a thumbnail of their powers, Geists can always see ghosts 24/7 and communicate with them. They can see how ghosts died, and see to some degree how people will die and how soon. This means Vampires and Mummies stand out somewhat. They can look into Twilight and interact with what is there, including spirits, but this power must be activated. They can sense when they are at a site where someone died and get an idea of how long ago, and sense the use of ghostly powers nearby. More importantly, they can &quot;bulwark&quot; damage by spending plasm, which essentially temporarily makes a wound not a wound, but makes it leak a sort of whitish fog. This means if they took, say, 6 lethal, they can bulwark it (and have a lot of smoke coming out) and take those levels over if they took 6 more damage, but otherwise leftover damage becomes bashing. They also cannot be forced into unconsciousness, they fight till death. They also rise from the dead after a short time when killed, but it damages their Morality maximum.<br /> <br /> Their powers focus around effects (Manifestations) and themes (keys). Marionette lets them control things, but while the Grave-Dirt key lets them make a homoculus with the Grave-Dirt Marionette, the passion key lets them control emotions, the Stigmata key lets them empower, weaken, and control ghosts, and the stillness key lets them raise zombies. This means their powers are somewhat exponential. Their most unusual power is Boneyard, which lets them observe events in an area and cause effects to happen, as if they were a ghost haunting. This can vary from making the temperature bitter cold and attacking with lightning outdoors (cold wind key) to making people lethargic or unaware of their surroundings (stillness key) to sensing and trapping ghosts (Stigmata key). They also have several powers to twist their bodies to battle forms (Caul and Shroud).<br /> <br /> Sin-Eaters are very powerful but their powers tend to either death with ghost or death or be profoundly negative. They can harm and damage better than positive effects. Sin-Eaters are overwhelmingly powerful at dealing with ghosts of any sort. They are also potent in the underworld, where they are unusually potent at navigating and can cross underworld rivers far easier than any other sort of being. Their other issue is their weakness: they don't have one. At best, kill them enough times they go away, but that is not exactly a weakness.<br /> <br /> They also have Ceremonies, sort of like Rites, they have access to. They actually do have a heal here, and a number of minor powers. These powers take time, from turns to hours and require focus.<br /> <br /> Sin-Eaters regain plasm at haunts. They also regain all plasm by helping a ghost move on. The food and drink of the Underworld restores plasm as well.<br /> <br /> Overall, Sin-Eaters tend to be very powerful, less than Mages and where they interact with Mummies depends on the stage of a Mummies cycle. However, much of that power is ghost focused or raw destructive power. There also tends to be some communication internationally so while they won't call down armies, messing with one Krewe can easily lead to Krewes all over the world hearing about you. Their relationship with their Krewe's ethos and mythology varies...some become relgious fanatics, some simply have an odd view of the world. They are one and all people who have risen from and now walk with the dead, and while usually content to their own activities protecting, helping, or exploiting the dead, they are dangerous to anger.<br /> <br /> Does that help? Can I clarify anything.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\">Forum Rules</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">This is my mod voice.</span> This is my goth voice.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/15731-geist-balance-of-shadows-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">[Geist: Balance of Shadows</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/ma